   "subject": "Re: [geomorph-r-package] Calculating species mean shape",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"line-break:after-white-space\">Mihajlo,<div><br></div><div>Here are two of many possible ways to do this, using the geomorph::pupfish data as an example.</div><div><br></div><div><div>&gt; library(geomorph)</div><div>&gt; data(&quot;pupfish&quot;) # data already have GPA performed</div><div>&gt; </div><div>&gt; group &lt;- interaction(pupfish$Pop, pupfish$Sex)</div><div>&gt; </div><div>&gt; # method 1 (produces 4 shapes, means for groups)</div><div>&gt; </div><div>&gt; subsets &lt;- coords.subset(pupfish$coords, group)</div><div>&gt; </div><div>&gt; means &lt;- lapply(subsets, mshape)</div><div>&gt; means &lt;- simplify2array(means)</div><div>&gt; </div><div>&gt; # method 2 (produces a matrix with 4 rows of means)</div><div>&gt; </div><div>&gt; fit &lt;- procD.lm(coords ~ group + 0, data = pupfish, </div><div>+                 print.progress = 0, iter = 0)</div><div>&gt; means2 &lt;- coef(fit)</div><div>&gt; </div><div>&gt; # consistency (make matrix into 3d array)</div><div>&gt; </div><div>&gt; means3 &lt;- arrayspecs(means2, p = 56, k = 2)</div><div>&gt;</div><div><div>&gt; dim(means)</div><div>[1] 56  2  4</div><div>&gt; dim(means2)</div><div>[1]   4 112</div><div>&gt; dim(means3)</div><div>[1] 56  2  4</div></div><div>&gt; </div><div>&gt; # Look at first mean from all methods</div><div>&gt; means[,,1]</div><div>              [,1]          [,2]</div><div> [1,] -0.036294242 -0.0679443538</div><div> [2,] -0.039057429 -0.0371420901</div><div> [3,]  0.099048079 -0.0661928367</div><div> [4,] -0.175052108  0.0004715517</div><div> [5,]  0.040550735  0.0536580991</div><div> [6,]  0.165215338  0.0273059678</div><div> [7,]  0.229413406  0.0209667222</div><div> [8,]  0.229474512 -0.0456403969</div><div> [9,]  0.140600252 -0.0516313039</div><div>[10,] -0.132042793 -0.0005377255</div><div>[11,] -0.161025824  0.0098325777</div><div>[12,] -0.148521266  0.0174386459</div><div>[13,] -0.134697146  0.0246232213</div><div>[14,] -0.120026613  0.0302320132</div><div