v> [93] -0.1082805988 -0.0676359900 -0.1311578801 -0.0587747433</div><div> [97] -0.1320761861  0.0168880338 -0.1195635859  0.0148077415</div><div>[101] -0.1113121373  0.0032175483 -0.1161834489 -0.0123691722</div><div>[105] -0.1330921898 -0.0207459089 -0.1483409355 -0.0144955519</div><div>[109] -0.1525370004  0.0005088135 -0.1438858560  0.0125317550</div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>The first method subsets the data into four groups and finds the mean of each group.  The second method uses a linear model without an intercept to estimate mean shapes, which are the coefficients.  There are other ways (using the aggregate function after making data into a matrix is another good one) but either of these are pretty efficient, using geomorph functions.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Mike</div><div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
