9016 -0.0751638785 -0.0597236155</div><div> [89] -0.0855006224 -0.0666617077 -0.0965163163 -0.0689598451</div><div> [93] -0.1082805988 -0.0676359900 -0.1311578801 -0.0587747433</div><div> [97] -0.1320761861  0.0168880338 -0.1195635859  0.0148077415</div><div>[101] -0.1113121373  0.0032175483 -0.1161834489 -0.0123691722</div><div>[105] -0.1330921898 -0.0207459089 -0.1483409355 -0.0144955519</div><div>[109] -0.1525370004  0.0005088135 -0.1438858560  0.0125317550</div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>The first method subsets the data into four groups and finds the mean of each group.  The second method uses a linear model without an intercept to estimate mean shapes, which are the coefficients.  There are other ways (using the aggregate function after making data into a matrix is another good one) but either of these are pretty efficient, using geomorph functions.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Mike</div><div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On Aug 15, 2024, at 2:47 PM, Mihajlo Milic &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mihajlo....@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br><div><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello everyone,<div>I have a shape dataset for 2504 individuals classified in one of 243 species. So each species has more than one individual and the number of individuals per species is unequal. Do you know how to calculate mean shapes for each species? I know this is more a general R question than a geomorph, but I believe someone had experience with this.<br></div><div>Mihajlo</div></div><div><br></div>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;geomorph R package&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-r-pack...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo view this discussion on the web, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/geomorph-r-package/CAJy6d1T%2Ba%2ByzCRfRhopnJHFHozgWun%3D-jE7